    "terminal_reason": "completed",
    "result_text": "I've implemented `search_remote` in `kb/remote.py`, and it works: against a real Streamable HTTP server it returned the right titles and sent `Authorization: Bearer <token>` on every request. `pytest -q` also passes (2 tests).\n\n**How it works:** In the pinned `mcp==2.2.0`, the HTTP client transport (`streamable_http_client`) has no `headers` argument. So I create an HTTP client with the SDK's `create_mcp_http_client(headers={\"Authorization\": f\"Bearer {token}\"})`, which keeps the SDK's default timeouts, and pass it in. Headers set on that client go out with every request the transport makes. The rest works like `search_local`: start the session, call `search` with `{\"query\": query}`, and return `split_titles(result)`. No dependencies were added.\n\n**End-to-end check:** I ran a throwaway script that started a real MCP server on localhost with a `search` tool. It rejected any request without the right header and logged the header on each request. The result was:\n- The returned titles were `['Result for keys', 'Onboarding']`, with blank lines dropped as in `search_local`.\n- All 6 HTTP requests carried `Bearer s3cret`: four POSTs, the GET stream, and the DELETE that closes the session.\n\n**One thing to note:** `create_mcp_http_client` comes from `mcp.shared._httpx_utils`. That module lists it in `__all__`, but the leading underscore marks it as internal to the SDK, so an SDK upgrade could move it. The alternative is building the client directly with `httpx2`, which would mean depending on a package the project doesn't list itself.\n\n**Cleanup needed:** Deleting the throwaway script needed your approval, so `_e2e_check.py` is still in the project root. You can delete it, or tell me to and approve the command."
  },
